## Unnecessary Re-renders in React

### 1. Reference Equality

React uses `Object.is` to compare props. Primitives compare by value; objects and functions compare by reference. A new reference on every render always fails equality, triggering a child re-render even when the data hasn't changed.

```jsx
// Every render creates a NEW object reference — child always re-renders
<Child style={{ color: 'red' }} />

// Every render creates a NEW function reference — same problem
<Child onClick={() => doSomething()} />
```

This is the root cause of most unnecessary re-renders. The fixes below are all ways to stabilize references.

---

### 2. The Three Memoization Tools

| Hook | Memoizes | Use When |
|---|---|---|
| `React.memo` | A component's output | A child re-renders with unchanged props |
| `useMemo` | A computed value | A calculation is expensive or produces an object/array passed as a prop |
| `useCallback` | A function reference | A function is passed as a prop to a memoized child |

**`React.memo`** — wraps a component; skips re-render if props are shallowly equal.

```jsx
const Child = React.memo(({ onClick, label }) => <button onClick={onClick}>{label}</button>);
```

**`useCallback`** — stabilizes a function reference across renders.

```jsx
const handleClick = useCallback(() => doSomething(id), [id]);
```

**`useMemo`** — stabilizes an object/array reference, or skips an expensive derivation.

```jsx
const config = useMemo(() => ({ color: 'red', size: 'large' }), []);
```

**When NOT to use them:** don't reach for these by default. They add overhead (memory, comparison cost). Profile first. The win only materializes when (a) the child is `React.memo`-wrapped and (b) the prop actually changes less often than the parent renders.

---

### 3. Context Provider Pitfalls

Every consumer re-renders when the context *value reference* changes — even if the data inside is identical.

```jsx
// BAD: new object on every render → all consumers re-render on every parent render
function Provider({ children }) {
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);
  return (
    <UserContext.Provider value={{ user, setUser }}>
      {children}
    </UserContext.Provider>
  );
}
```

**Fix 1 — memoize the value:**
```jsx
const value = useMemo(() => ({ user, setUser }), [user]);
```

**Fix 2 (better) — split state and dispatch into separate contexts.** Dispatch never changes; state changes only when data changes. Consumers subscribe only to what they need.

```jsx
const UserStateContext = createContext();
const UserDispatchContext = createContext();

function Provider({ children }) {
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);
  return (
    <UserDispatchContext.Provider value={setUser}>  {/* stable */}
      <UserStateContext.Provider value={user}>       {/* changes with data */}
        {children}
      </UserStateContext.Provider>
    </UserDispatchContext.Provider>
  );
}
```

---

### 4. Before / After

**Before** — `List` re-renders on every `Page` render, even though its props don't change meaningfully.

```jsx
function Page() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  // New reference every render
  const filters = { active: true };
  const handleSelect = (id) => console.log(id);

  return (
    <>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>Count: {count}</button>
      <List filters={filters} onSelect={handleSelect} />
    </>
  );
}

function List({ filters, onSelect }) {
  console.log('List rendered'); // fires on every count increment
  return <div>...</div>;
}
```

**After** — `List` only re-renders when its props actually change.

```jsx
function Page() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  // Stable references — only re-created when dependencies change
  const filters = useMemo(() => ({ active: true }), []);
  const handleSelect = useCallback((id) => console.log(id), []);

  return (
    <>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>Count: {count}</button>
      <List filters={filters} onSelect={handleSelect} />
    </>
  );
}

const List = React.memo(function List({ filters, onSelect }) {
  console.log('List rendered'); // only fires when filters or onSelect change
  return <div>...</div>;
});
```

**The pattern requires all three pieces together:** `React.memo` on the child + stable references for object props (`useMemo`) + stable references for function props (`useCallback`). Any one missing breaks the optimization.
